{"name":"Singapore","slug":"singapore","region":"Southeast Asia & Oceania","lat":1.35,"lon":103.82,"seasons":{"DJF":{"season_name":"Dec 2026–Feb 2027","years":[1951,1952,1953,1954,1955,1956,1957,1958,1959,1960,1961,1962,1963,1964,1965,1966,1967,1968,1969,1970,1971,1972,1973,1974,1975,1976,1977,1978,1979,1980,1981,1982,1983,1984,1985,1986,1987,1988,1989,1990,1991,1992,1993,1994,1995,1996,1997,1998,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2005,2006,2007,2008,2009,2010,2011,2012,2013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024,2025],"roni_lead":[-0.38,0.98,0.04,0.84,-0.31,-1.31,-0.14,1.16,0.24,0.04,0.08,-0.16,-0.3,1.06,-0.64,1.86,-0.03,-0.08,0.56,0.67,-0.57,-0.4,1.82,-1.63,-0.45,-0.95,1.04,0.89,-0.08,0.52,-0.06,-0.17,2.03,-0.9,-0.61,-0.18,1.0,1.2,-1.71,-0.23,0.27,1.2,0.1,0.46,0.97,-0.93,-0.25,2.22,-1.33,-1.06,-0.55,-0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Dec–Feb in Singapore has warmed by about 0.25 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.8 °C in all, and the hottest days have warmed faster than the coldest (about 0.25 vs 0.2 °C per decade). There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Dec–Feb tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 35% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 1.5 times a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 18 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Dec–Feb this warm would come about once every 21 years. Dec–Feb rainfall has risen by about 5% per decade. El Niño has a modest but consistent effect toward a wetter season with more rainy days, and this year's shift is a noticeable tilt. Relative humidity has fallen by about 0.3 points per decade, roughly 2 points since 1951; because warmer air holds more moisture, this does not necessarily mean the air is drier. El Niño has a modest but consistent effect toward a more humid Dec–Feb, and this year's shift is substantial. For Dec 2026–Feb 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is very likely (more than 95% chance), about 0.9 °C above the 1991–2020 average; a wetter-than-normal season is likely (about 70% chance). This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":26.2049,"trend_per_decade":0.2389,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":1.7914,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.194,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","survives_fdr":true,"fraction_explained":0.3384,"intrinsic_sd":0.2543,"effect_this_season":0.4269,"effect_ratio":1.6788,"size":"Dominant","size_typical_el_nino":"Dominant","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":21.0,"expected_anomaly_2027":0.9305,"p_below":0.0,"p_near":0.0008,"p_above":0.9992,"forecast_words":"Very likely (>95%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":true,"years_of_warming_equivalent":17.8711,"values":[25.043,25.661,25.274,25.44,24.966,24.898,25.109,25.557,25.507,25.427,25.422,25.107,24.909,25.534,24.776,25.411,25.163,25.084,25.677,25.363,25.025,25.179,25.739,24.972,25.437,25.294,25.28,25.778,25.51,25.605,25.494,25.388,25.871,24.945,25.662,25.387,25.497,25.997,25.555,25.735,25.763,25.544,25.616,25.976,25.782,25.437,25.995,26.685,26.073,25.825,25.997,25.961,26.31,26.227,26.355,26.354,26.04,25.961,26.172,26.74,25.971,26.223,26.241,26.103,26.198,27.176,26.891,26.476,27.15,26.907,26.411,26.76,26.265,27.039,26.602],"forecast_mean":27.1354,"forecast_sd":0.2766,"fit":{"a2027":26.7085,"b":0.194,"c":0.0239,"tercile_low":25.9883,"tercile_high":26.2638},"percentiles":{"p05":{"trend_per_decade":0.1773,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.1724,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":0.3793,"lines_cross":false},"p50":{"trend_per_decade":0.2406,"trend_p":0.0,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.1816,"enso_p":0.0,"confidence":"Strong","shift_this_season":0.3995,"lines_cross":false},"p95"